
%9Syntax:%9

@SYNTAX:e2e@

%9Parameters:%9

    HELP:        A guide with the state of the current window and the list
                 of subcommands (same as /E2E without arguments).
    ON:          Turns encryption on in the current channel or query
                 (normal mode).
    OFF:         Turns encryption off; the mode is remembered. In a query
                 only for the person's current address (or, when it is
                 not known, the one this query had); other addresses of
                 that nick with encryption on are listed. With an
                 ident@host or a channel name: turns it off for that
                 address or channel, from any window.
    MODE:        Sets the mode and also turns encryption ON. Without an
                 argument: normal; "auto" = auto-accept.
                   normal       a key request waits for /E2E ACCEPT,
                                unless you accepted that person here
                                before,
                   auto-accept  the key is sent back automatically,
                   quiet        requests from people whose key you do
                                not have are dropped silently; the
                                others wait for /E2E ACCEPT.
    FINGERPRINT: The full fingerprint of your key (creates the key if it
                 does not exist).
    STATUS:      Fingerprint, number of incoming sessions and number of
                 channels or queries with encryption on, then where it is
                 on as <network>:<name> (old entries of no network by
                 their name alone, with a hint to /E2E ADOPT).
    LIST:        Sessions in the current channel or query: ident@host,
                 channel, start of the fingerprint, status (trusted,
                 pending, revoked, or "key held, not accepted": you can
                 read them, but they do not get your key). -all: the whole
                 keyring - known people and all sessions. Channels and
                 queries are shown as <network>:<name>. In a window also
                 the key requests waiting there ("pending: <nick>
                 (<ident@host>)").
    PENDING:     Every key request waiting for /E2E ACCEPT, on every
                 network: nick, ident@host, <network>:<name>, the start of
                 the fingerprint of the key and how long it has waited.
    HANDSHAKE:   Sends the person a key request (KEYREQ) for the current
                 channel or query; encryption must be on here and their
                 ident@host known (a common channel, a query).
    ACCEPT:      Accepts the person: sends your key back (KEYRSP) to their
                 pending request and requests theirs in return; their
                 later requests here are answered without asking. Without
                 an argument: the only request waiting in this window
                 (with several they are listed - name one). With a nick:
                 the request waiting here from that nick (compared as the
                 server compares nicks); when the nick names requests of
                 two addresses, none is accepted - they are listed, name
                 one by its ident@host. With an ident@host: in a channel
                 window the channel's request of that address, if one
                 waits there; otherwise - from any window of the
                 network - the request of that address for a query with
                 it. Only requests of this window's channel or query, or
                 of that address's query, on this network are ever taken.
                 Without a pending request, a nick marks their session
                 here (an ident@host: their query's session) as trusted
                 and accepts them - only when a real key from them is
                 stored. A query's key goes only to that query's own
                 address: a request from anyone else for it is damaged
                 (rpe2e.pl kept such requests before) - listed by
                 /E2E PENDING and /E2E ACCEPT, never accepted, removed by
                 /E2E DECLINE alone in that query. Such an address that was
                 accepted already (with rpe2e.pl) is damaged too: it may
                 hold your key of the query, so that key rotates by itself
                 with your next message there (you are told once) and the
                 new one never reaches it; /E2E LIST shows it, and /E2E
                 DECLINE <ident@host> (or alone) in that query removes it.
    DECLINE:     Declines a pending exchange, chosen as for ACCEPT; an
                 existing session gets the revoked status and the person
                 is no longer accepted.
    REVOKE:      Withdraws trust in the current channel or query and
                 schedules a rotation of your key, so that person can no
                 longer read along; they are no longer accepted.
    UNREVOKE:    Restores trust in their session (only when a real key
                 from them is stored); to give them your key again,
                 /E2E ACCEPT them.
    FORGET:      Removes the person's sessions in the current channel or
                 query; with -all (at the start or at the end) - everything
                 about them from the keyring.
    VERIFY:      Shows the start of your fingerprint and of the person's -
                 to compare over another channel (e.g. by phone).
    REVERIFY:    After the person's key has changed (the exchange is then
                 blocked), accepts the new key; without a recorded change
                 it clears the old data and a new exchange is needed.
    ROTATE:      Schedules a rotation of your key in the current channel
                 or query; the new key is created with the next message and
                 sent to trusted people.
    EXPORT:      Writes the whole keyring, including private keys, to a
                 new JSON file (mode 0600); an existing file or a symlink
                 is never overwritten.
    IMPORT:      REPLACES the whole keyring with the file's contents (no
                 merging); the previous one is kept as
                 keyring.json.bak-<time>.
    AUTOTRUST:   Without an argument or with LIST - the list of rules. ADD
                 adds a rule: the scope is "global" or an exact channel
                 name (or "@ident@host" of a query), the pattern is
                 ident@host with * and ? (case-insensitive); matching
                 people are accepted automatically. A channel added here
                 is the one of the window's network (listed as
                 <network>:<name>; a window without a server cannot add
                 one); rules from rpe2e.pl name a channel of every
                 network (listed with "(every network)"). REMOVE removes
                 the rules with that pattern.
    ADOPT:       Binds the old entries of the current channel or query
                 (from rpe2e.pl, which keeps no networks apart) to the
                 window's network; in a query also the
                 person's keys for their messages to you. Needed when
                 erssi uses several networks: until then E2E there is
                 refused. When another network took the old entries
                 over already, they stay there and this network gets
                 encryption of its own - always turned on, in normal
                 mode (keys are exchanged anew; /E2E OFF afterwards
                 sends in clear text there). When the network has
                 entries of its own for the name, nothing changes (to
                 use the old ones there instead: /E2E EXPORT, remove
                 that network's entries for the name from the file,
                 /E2E IMPORT it, then /E2E ADOPT).
    RESET:       Only after a corrupt keyring was moved aside (see below):
                 starts a new keyring with a new identity. Encryption is
                 then off everywhere until /E2E ON, and your peers see a
                 new key (/E2E REVERIFY on their side).

%9Description:%9

    RPE2E end-to-end encryption (XChaCha20-Poly1305, a separate key per
    sender and channel or query), compatible with repartee, the rpe2e.pl
    script and the WeeChat rpe2e.py script. Built into erssi as the e2e
    module: /load e2e loads it (in lower case: the module's file name is
    built from the word as typed). To load it at every start, add e2e to the
    autoload_modules setting (/SET autoload_modules followed by the current
    list and e2e, in lower case too). A module loaded only by /LOAD is not
    loaded again after a restart or /UPGRADE, and until it is, messages to
    channels and queries with E2E on are sent in clear text. The module says
    so: when it is loaded and at /E2E ON (with the /SET to run) while e2e is
    not in autoload_modules, at /UPGRADE then while E2E is on anywhere
    (printed just before the restart, so it may be gone from the screen
    afterwards; a log of that window keeps it), and at /unload e2e while E2E
    is on anywhere or the keyring is lost. Keys are stored in
    ~/.erssi/rpe2e/keyring.json - the file rpe2e.pl uses, so moving from the
    script needs no new keys.

    If the rpe2e.pl script is loaded too, the module stays inactive and
    says so; unload the script (/SCRIPT UNLOAD rpe2e, and remove it from
    scripts/autorun) to use the module. The script does not know the
    module's per-network entries (see Networks), so it would send a
    channel or query that only the module set up in clear text: while
    the script is loaded the module still stops such messages (they are
    NOT sent, and you are told), lets bot commands out with a warning,
    and warns once that the keyring has such entries. Going back to
    rpe2e.pl after the module wrote them is a downgrade - unload the
    script to send there encrypted; /E2E EXPORT keeps a copy.

    Subcommands work in the context of the active window: in a channel
    window it is the channel, in a query window - the person identified by
    ident@host. Encryption in a query requires the other side's ident@host
    to be known (an open query, a common channel or an earlier exchange)
    and your own, which is learned via WHOIS after connecting - until then
    some commands ask you to retry. A nick in ACCEPT and DECLINE names a
    request waiting in the window first; nicks in REVOKE, VERIFY etc. are
    resolved using the data stored in the keyring.

    Once enabled, every PRIVMSG to the channel or query (plain text, /ME,
    /MSG, /SAY, /QUOTE PRIVMSG in any case, scripts, other frontends) is
    encrypted. When encryption is not possible, the message is NOT sent,
    although it is shown locally, and an [E2E] message is printed; a line
    to several targets at once (#a,#b) is not sent when one of them is
    encrypted. Exceptions: on a channel, lines starting with '.' or '!'
    followed by a letter (bot commands such as .op or !seen) are sent in
    plain text, with a warning - "..." or "!!" are encrypted - and CTCPs
    other than ACTION are always sent in plain text. NOTICEs are not
    encrypted.

    An encrypted message from a person without a trusted session is
    hidden, and a key request is sent to them (at most every 30 s) if
    encryption is on here; their answer lets you read them. Your own key
    goes only to people you accepted: /E2E ACCEPT, an AUTOTRUST rule or
    auto-accept mode. Having their key is not enough - so in normal mode
    each of you accepts the other once. A request for a channel is
    announced in its window as "Pending key exchange from <nick>
    (<ident@host>) for <network>:<name>" - reply there with /E2E ACCEPT
    or /E2E DECLINE (alone, or with the ident@host shown); one for a query
    as "... for a query", answered with /E2E ACCEPT <ident@host> from any
    window of that network. Asked again, a request is announced at most
    every 5 minutes, and /E2E PENDING lists every request that waits. Later
    requests from an accepted person are answered at once. Requests for a
    channel or query where encryption is off are ignored, a request for a
    query must come from the person it names, and each kind of request
    (KEYREQ, KEYRSP, REKEY) from one person for one context is handled at
    most every 10 s. A key change announced by a person (REKEY) only
    replaces a key you already have from them, and each one is taken once
    (after 1024 of them from one person in one context the next key change
    goes through a new key request instead). When your own key changes, only
    people you accepted get the new one. At most 64 requests wait for /E2E
    ACCEPT, at most 256 of your own key requests wait for an answer and at
    most 256 people you never accepted are remembered, with their keys; the
    oldest go first (people you accepted are always kept). Messages of the
    module are prefixed with [E2E]; with /SET e2e_debug ON it also explains
    what it drops. Line breaks in a decrypted message are shown as spaces.

    A message to a nick is not sent when encryption is on for that nick
    at another ident@host (a new host, or someone else took the nick), or
    when its address is not known at the moment (no query, no common
    channel): /E2E ON in the query encrypts to the new address after a
    new key exchange, /E2E OFF <ident@host> turns encryption off for the
    old one.
    A raw line holding several IRC lines (CR/LF, from a script) is not
    sent when one of them is a PRIVMSG to an encrypted conversation, and
    an encrypted line that would be longer than the server allows (a very
    long channel name) is not sent either. /UPGRADE sends the messages
    still waiting in the flood queue through the encryption as well.

    Long messages: an encrypted message is sent in parts (chunks) of up to
    180 bytes, each a line of its own, and they leave through the flood
    queue like other commands (cmds_max_at_once, cmd_queue_speed; irssi
    also waits 2 s and 1 s per 100 bytes after a long line), so a message
    of several parts can take some seconds to go out - a line you type is
    split by irssi at the server's line limit first, and each of those
    lines is encrypted on its own. Later messages to that network wait
    behind the parts and leave in the order typed; each is encrypted when
    its turn comes, so after /E2E OFF the messages still waiting go out in
    clear text. Other commands (PART, NICK, QUIT, TOPIC, NOTICEs) do not
    wait: they leave before the parts still waiting - after /PART the rest
    of a message to a +n channel is rejected by the server. On disconnect
    or /E2E RESET the parts not sent yet are dropped and the number is
    shown in the status window (the receiver gets an incomplete message).
    /unload e2e drops them too, and the messages in irssi's own flood
    queue that the module would encrypt or refuse (they would leave in
    clear text without it); clear text it would let pass stays queued.
    /UPGRADE sends everything waiting at once, past the flood limit,
    before it restarts. When rpe2e.pl is loaded the script encrypts, and
    what still waits in the module is dropped the same way.

    Statusbar: the e2e item shows the E2E state of the active channel or
    query - "e2e off"; "E2E" (on, a key from someone to read); "E2E
    waiting for keys" (on, no key from anyone yet); "E2E blocked" (not
    sent: an old entry of unknown network, or the query's nick has E2E
    on at another address); "E2E keyring error" - followed by "N to
    accept" while N key requests wait there for /E2E ACCEPT. Nothing in
    other windows, and nothing while rpe2e.pl is loaded. It is not in the
    shipped bars; add it with /STATUSBAR ADDITEM -after window e2e <bar>
    (the shipped bar with "window" is "info"). The texts are the formats
    e2e_sb_off, e2e_sb_on, e2e_sb_waiting, e2e_sb_blocked, e2e_sb_error and
    e2e_sb_pending (/FORMAT, themes). A keyring changed by another program
    (rpe2e.pl in another erssi) is shown at the next window switch or the
    next message sent.

    Keyrings: a keyring that cannot be read is never overwritten, and
    messages are not sent until it can be read again. One that is not
    valid JSON is moved aside to keyring.json.corrupt-<time>; the E2E
    state is then LOST: nothing is sent to any channel or query (except
    bot commands) and nothing is saved, also after a restart, until you
    run /E2E RESET (a new keyring) or /E2E IMPORT a keyring export.
    Which people you accepted is stored in keyring.json as "accepted"
    (rpe2e.pl keeps it, exports do not carry it): with a keyring from
    rpe2e.pl, repartee or an import, a person asking for your key again
    needs one /E2E ACCEPT, and does not get your new key when it rotates
    before that.

    Channel names are compared as the server's CASEMAPPING compares
    them: ascii, rfc1459 and strict-rfc1459 exactly (rfc1459 when the
    server names none), and Latin-1 letters as servers of the ircu
    family fold them; rfc7613, rfc8265 and utf8 with a wider fold (NFKC,
    case folding and the rfc1459 specials), which may refuse a message
    to a channel the server keeps apart, but never lets one out in clear
    text to a channel with E2E on. Until the server has named its
    CASEMAPPING, or ended its MOTD without one, the wider fold is used.
    A name the wider fold cannot decide - not valid UTF-8 or holding an
    unassigned character on such a server, and any non-ASCII name on a
    server whose CASEMAPPING erssi does not know (it may be a byte code
    page such as cp1251) - is not sent in clear text while E2E is on for
    any channel of that network (and while E2E is on for such a name,
    other names there that cannot be told apart from it get no clear
    text either). On IRCnet, !name is refused while E2E is on for a
    !XXXXXname of that name. The nick of a query with E2E on, also one
    rpe2e.pl keeps by nick, is compared the same way.

    A message of a peer that opens only under the key of a channel the
    wider fold takes for this one, though some servers keep the two
    apart (for example #a[b and #a{b), is shown with "(E2E key of
    #a[b)": the server may have moved it from that channel.

    Known limitations: a server that names rfc1459, ascii or
    strict-rfc1459 but folds the bytes of another code page (koi8-r,
    cp1251) is not covered: such names may be one channel to it and two
    to erssi. NOTICEs, CTCPs other than ACTION, bot commands and IRCv3
    message tags are never encrypted. Nicks in ACCEPT, REVOKE, VERIFY
    etc. are resolved with the last nick stored in the keyring, which
    may meanwhile belong to someone else - check the ident@host the
    command prints. A keyring written with real Unicode (repartee's
    export) that stores a nick whose letters all fit Latin-1 (kótek) is
    read as Latin-1 bytes, so neither erssi nor rpe2e.pl finds that nick
    by it (one with other letters, żbik, is found). As in repartee, the
    server can replay an encrypted message within 5 minutes, and drop or
    reorder the parts of a long one. A key request carries no ident@host:
    someone who forwards your peer's request before the peer's first
    contact with you ties that key to their own address, and the peer's
    own request is then held back as a changed address until /E2E REVERIFY
    (nobody gets a key that way). The rpe2e.pl script is recognised by its
    file name (rpe2e*.pl).

    Networks: keys, sessions and settings are kept per network - the
    chatnet name of the server, otherwise its tag - so #test on one
    network and #test on another are two conversations with keys of
    their own (in keyring.json as "<network>" U+001F "#test"; on the wire
    only the bare name is used, as rpe2e.pl and repartee do). A keyring from
    rpe2e.pl (one entry per name, for any network) is used as it is while
    erssi uses one network (counting the servers connected, connecting,
    waiting to reconnect and set to autoconnect) - nothing to do - and
    becomes that network's with the first message you send there (or /E2E
    ADOPT) - messages and key requests from others never move it.
    In a query, your first message to the person also takes along the
    keys of their messages to you; those of other people stay old until
    you write to them while erssi uses one network - on several networks
    their messages are not read until /E2E ADOPT.
    With several networks erssi cannot know which network an old entry
    belongs to: a message to such a channel or query is NOT sent (you are
    told why), and nothing is decrypted or answered for it there, until
    /E2E ADOPT in its window binds the old entries to that network, or
    /E2E OFF turns encryption off for it on that network. Once old entries
    belong to one network, the same name on any other network is refused
    the same way until it is set up there (/E2E ADOPT, /E2E OFF); the
    entries of one network never move to another. EXPORT and IMPORT keep
    the per-network entries as they are (repartee's exports use the same
    form).
    A network without a chatnet is labelled by its server tag, which can
    change when tags collide - set a chatnet for stable E2E scopes.
    A channel or address whose name holds the control character U+001F
    cannot be kept per network: nothing is encrypted for it, and a
    message to it is not sent while an old entry may have encryption on.

    EXPORT and IMPORT do not expand '~' - give a full path.

%9Examples:%9

    /E2E
    /E2E ON
    /E2E PENDING
    /E2E ACCEPT
    /E2E ACCEPT alice
    /E2E DECLINE mallory@host.example.org
    /E2E VERIFY alice
    /E2E MODE auto-accept
    /E2E LIST -all
    /E2E FORGET -all bob
    /E2E AUTOTRUST ADD global *@*.example.org
    /E2E ADOPT
    /E2E EXPORT /path/to/e2e-keyring.json
    /E2E RESET
    /E2E OFF bob@old.example.org

%9See also:%9 MSG, QUERY, LOAD, STATUSBAR, FORMAT

